Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (you can also use an existing UI element).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` with a set of options that give a smooth, natural animation.  
3. (Optional) **Keep the map readable** while the camera moves – e.g., keep labels legible on imagery by using a dark halo, and make sure the animation does not hide important symbols.

---

## 1️⃣ Minimal HTML

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it accessible */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">Fly to SF</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="fly-to-demo.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Why the button?*  
- It’s focusable (`<button>`), has an `aria-label`, and a visible focus ring – good for keyboard and screen‑reader users.  
- The same pattern works for any UI element (toolbar, dropdown, list item, etc.).

---

## 2️⃣ JavaScript – `fly-to-demo.js`

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map (satellite imagery + vector labels)
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // imagery style with vector labels
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (initial view)
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,
  bearing: -17,
  // Make sure the style’s label colors work on imagery:
  // (the demo style already uses white text + dark halo)
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Helper: smoothFlyTo – a thin wrapper around map.flyTo()
// ---------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Fly the camera to a target location with a pleasant easing curve.
 *
 * @param {Object} opts
 *   @property {Array<number>} center   [lon, lat] in EPSG:4326
 *   @property {number}        zoom     (optional) target zoom level
 *   @property {number}        bearing  (optional) target bearing in degrees
 *   @property {number}        pitch    (optional) target pitch in degrees
 *   @property {number}        duration (optional) ms; default 1500
 *   @property {number}        curve    (optional) animation curvature, default 1.42
 *   @property {boolean}       essential (optional) true → animation runs even if user prefers‑reduced‑motion is off
 */
function smoothFlyTo({center, zoom, bearing, pitch, duration = 1500, curve = 1.42, essential = true}) {
  // Respect the OS “reduce motion” setting unless you explicitly need the animation.
  // Setting `essential: true` tells the browser that the animation is not purely decorative.
  // If you want to *skip* the animation for users who prefer reduced motion, set essential:false.
  map.flyTo({
    center,
    zoom,
    bearing,
    pitch,
    duration,
    curve,
    essential,
    // Optional: add a custom easing function (MapLibre uses a cubic‑bezier internally)
    // easing: t => t < 0.5 ? 2*t*t : -1+(4-2*t)*t   // “ease‑in‑out” – you can omit this line.
  });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Wire the button click
// ---------------------------------------------------
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Example target: Golden Gate Bridge
  const target = {
    center: [-122.478255, 37.819932], // lon, lat
    zoom: 15,
    bearing: 0,
    pitch: 60,
    duration: 2000,   // 2 seconds – feels smooth but not sluggish
    curve: 1.5        // slightly more “zoom‑out‑then‑in” feel
  };
  smoothFlyTo(target);
});
```

### What the options do

| Option | Effect | Typical values |
|--------|--------|----------------|
| `center` | Geographic point to move to | `[lon, lat]` in EPSG:4326 |
| `zoom`   | Final zoom level (0‑22) | 12‑16 for city‑scale, 5‑8 for regional |
| `bearing`| Rotation around north (°) | 0‑360; 0 = north‑up |
| `pitch`  | Tilt of the camera (°) | 0‑85; 45‑60 feels 3‑D on imagery |
| `duration`| How long the animation lasts (ms) | 1000‑2500 for a “smooth” feel |
| `curve`  | Controls the “zoom‑out‑then‑in” curvature. 1.42 is the default; >1.5 gives a more pronounced “fly‑over” effect. |
| `essential`| If `true`, the animation runs even when the user has “reduce motion” disabled. Set to `false` to automatically skip the animation for users who prefer reduced motion. |

---

## 3️⃣ Why `flyTo` (instead of `jumpTo`)

* `flyTo` interpolates **both position and zoom** using a smooth Bézier curve, mimicking the classic “flight” animation you see in Mapbox GL and MapLibre demos.  
* It automatically **keeps labels readable**: the map’s label halo (`text-halo-color`) stays the same while the camera moves, so the contrast does not flicker.  
* The animation respects the **`essential` flag**, which is the accessibility‑friendly way to honor the user’s “reduce motion” preference.

---

## 4️⃣ Extra Tips for Real‑World Projects

| Situation | Recommendation |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Multiple buttons (different destinations)** | Store each destination in a small lookup table and call `smoothFlyTo()` with the appropriate object. |
| **Fly to a feature that you just selected on a sidebar** | Use the feature’s `bbox` to compute a `center` and a `zoom` that fits it: <br>`map.fitBounds(bbox, {padding: 40, duration: 1200});` – `fitBounds` internally uses the same animation engine. |
| **You need to keep a layer (e.g., a highlighted polygon) on top while flying** | Ensure that layer is **above** the basemap’s symbol layers (see the “Style Layer Ordering” section of the cartography skill). Insert it after the first `symbol` layer or give it a higher `z-index` via `layout: { "symbol-sort-key": 1 }`. |
| **You want a “bounce‑back” effect after the fly** | Chain a second `flyTo` in the `moveend` event: <br>`map.once('moveend', () => map.flyTo({center, zoom: zoom-0.5, duration: 300, curve: 0.8}));` |
| **You need to disable user interaction while the camera moves** | `map.dragPan.disable(); map.scrollZoom.disable();` before the call, then re‑enable in the `moveend` handler. |
| **You are using a custom sprite for a “target” marker** | Add a temporary SDF icon at the destination, give it an `icon-halo-color`/`icon-halo-width` so it stays visible over any background, then remove it after the animation. |

---

## 5️⃣ Full‑page Demo (run it locally)

If you copy the HTML + JS above into a folder and open `index.html` in a browser, you’ll see a map centered on San Francisco. Clicking **“Fly to SF”** animates the camera to the Golden Gate Bridge with a smooth 2‑second flight, a 60° pitch, and a dark halo‑backed label set that stays legible over the satellite imagery.

---

### TL;DR Code Snippet

```js
// One‑liner you can drop into any existing MapLibre app:
document.getElementById('myBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  map.flyTo({
    center: [-122.478255, 37.819932],
    zoom: 15,
    bearing: 0,
    pitch: 60,
    duration: 2000,
    curve: 1.5,
    essential: true   // set false to respect "reduce motion"
  });
});
```

That’s all you need for a smooth, accessible camera transition after a button click! 🚀
